"""Table captions, verbatim from the manuscript (J. Rheol. version)."""
CAPTIONS = {'converse_table': 'Test of the condition of Sec.~\\ref{sec:converse}. The base model is the Giesekus model written for the stress, $\\bm A=\\bm\\sigma$: $\\dot{\\bm\\sigma}-\\bm L\\!\\cdot\\!\\bm\\sigma-\\bm\\sigma\\!\\cdot\\!\\bm L^\\T=2\\bm D-\\bm R$ with $\\bm R=\\bm\\sigma+\\alpha_{\\rm G}\\bm\\sigma^2$, $\\alpha_{\\rm G}=0.2$, in units $\\eta_0=\\tau=1$. Each tensorial term is added to $\\bm R$ with coefficient $\\kappa=0.2$ (the modified spin replaces $\\bm W$; a modified relaxation rate multiplies $\\bm R$); $[\\bm A,\\bm X]=\\bm A\\!\\cdot\\!\\bm X-\\bm X\\!\\cdot\\!\\bm A$ and $\\bm D_{\\!A}$ is the part of $\\bm D$ coaxial with $\\bm A$, Eq.~\\eqref{DA}. The second column is the largest mismatch between the principal stresses in the three-dimensional flows I and II of Table~\\ref{tab:3d} and those of the mapped shear-free flows, computed independently, relative to the largest principal stress of the shear-free flow.', 'flows_table': 'Three-dimensional steady flows mapped onto shear-free flows. Flow I: $\\bm L=2\\,\\bm e_1\\bm e_2+\\operatorname{diag}(-0.15,-0.15,0.3)$ (shear with stretching along the vorticity axis). Flow II: a generic velocity gradient with all components of $\\bm D$ and $\\bm W$ nonzero, $L_{11},L_{12},L_{13};L_{22},L_{23};L_{31},L_{33}=0.2,1.5,0.7;-0.3,0.9;0.4,0.1$. Rates in units of $1/\\tau$. The last column is the largest difference between the principal stresses of the flow and of the shear-free flow $\\operatorname{diag}(d_i)$ computed independently from rest, relative to the largest principal stress.', 'nemd_table': 'Third-order-fluid analysis of the NEMD results of \\cite{Daivis2007} for bead-rod chains of $N$ sites (reduced Lennard-Jones units). $\\bar\\tau=\\Psi_{1,0}/2\\eta_0$. $b_3$ is obtained by adding their coefficient $(b_3-2b_{12}+2b_{1:11})$ from $\\eta_{P1}$ and $2(b_{12}-b_{1:11})$ from $\\eta(\\gd)$; $b_2^2/b_1=\\Psi_{1,0}^2/4\\eta_0$. The fifth column, $\\langle\\tau\\rangle^2/\\langle\\tau^2\\rangle=b_2^2/b_1b_3$, is the fraction of the linear-viscoelastic part $16b_3\\ed^2$ of the leading rise of $\\eta_{P1}$ that the composite reproduces. The last two columns compare the measured coefficient of $16\\ed^2$ in $\\eta_{P1}$ with the one implied by the composite, $b_2^2/b_1-2(b_{12}-b_{1:11})$. Uncertainties are propagated from the standard errors quoted by \\cite{Daivis2007}.', 'bd_table': 'Brownian dynamics of FENE dumbbells without closure. Steady shear at $\\mathrm{Wi}=\\gd\\lambda_H$ gives $\\eta$, $N_1$, the effective rate $\\eeff$ and the composite $\\eta_{[P1]}$; a separate simulation of planar extension at $\\ed=\\eeff$ gives the true $\\eta_{P1}$. Stresses in units of $nkT$, times in units of $\\lambda_H$. Statistical standard errors of the two viscosities are below $0.3\\%$ (see Appendix~\\ref{app:num} for caveats). The lower block repeats $\\mathrm{Wi}=10$ with a halved time step, with a different random seed, and for other values of $b$.', 'coax_table': 'FENE dumbbells ($b=50$) in steady shear: orientation angles of the stress, $\\chi_\\sigma$, and of the second moment $\\bm C=\\langle\\bm Q\\bm Q\\rangle$, $\\chi_C$ (degrees; statistical errors below $0.15^\\circ$); ratio of the Peterlin estimate of the stress computed from the \\emph{measured} $\\bm C$ to the true stress; mean square extension and fraction $P_{Q^2>b/2}$ of dumbbells with $Q^2>b/2$.'}
